  "summary": "Earthen mound architecture characterized by flat-topped pyramids used for ceremonial platforms and elite residences.",
  "description": "Features large stepped platforms built from earth, clay, and shell midden, often with ramps and staircases leading to summit structures. Developed as Mississippian societies became more complex, with mound centers serving as political and religious hubs.",
  "culturalContext": "The Mississippian platform mound (900-1600 CE) — Cahokia's Monks Mound (largest earthen structure in the Americas: 30m high, 290m long), Moundville, Etowah — staged Mississippian cosmology: flat-topped earthen pyramids bearing temples and elite residences above plazas, aligning solstice sunrises and ranking society vertically. Built basket-load by basket-load, they anchored the Southeastern Ceremonial Complex's \"Southern Cult\" iconography; surviving mounds are tribal nations' heritage landmarks and archaeological climate records.",
